345. Why did Odysseus tell the Phaeacians that he had blinded the Cyclops, who was a son of Poseidon, when they too were descendants of Poseidon? Aristotle, resolving this, says that he knew they were enemies of the Cyclops. For he says that, driven out by the Cyclopes, they came to Scheria (Od. 6.4): "Who once dwelt in broad Hypereia,
near the Cyclopes, overbearing men,
who used to harm them, and were stronger in might;
from there godlike Nausithous removed them and led them,
and settled them in Scheria, far from bread-eating men."
